  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two ways molecules can be attached to a surface: ordinary supramolecular adsorption and mechanical interlocking. At the top, ring-shaped molecules are shown approaching a rod-like axle fixed to a surface. In the supramolecular case, a ring binds at a specific site through strong interactions, producing a localized, conventional host–guest complex. In the mechanically interlocked case, bulky stopper-like groups and rings threaded on the axle create a structure that is held together mainly by topology rather than by a single strong binding interaction, so the contact is described as weakly interacting.\n\nThe lower part explains the energetic consequence. For chemisorption, the energy profile drops into one deep well, indicating formation of a stable low-energy state after crossing a barrier. For mechanisorption, the profile has multiple wells separated by barriers, and the final bound arrangement appears to remain in a higher-energy metastable state. Arrows on the barriers suggest mechanical trapping and restricted motion along the axle. Overall, the image emphasizes that mechanical interlocking can immobilize molecules on a surface even without strong chemical bonding, but with a different, less thermodynamically favorable energy landscape.",
